---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const macroCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Carbohydrates — 4 kcal/g', note: 'The preferred fuel: sugars and starches are broken to glucose, the brain\'s default substrate. Fibre is the carbohydrate we digest by not digesting it.' },
    { math: '2 · Protein — 4 kcal/g', note: 'Twenty amino acids, nine of them essential. Protein builds and repairs; the body has no storage tank, so supply must be daily.' },
    { math: '3 · Fat — 9 kcal/g', note: 'The dense fuel and the carrier for vitamins A, D, E and K. Essential fatty acids (linoleic, alpha-linolenic) must come from food.' },
    { math: '4 · Micronutrients — zero kcal, non-negotiable', note: 'Vitamins and minerals run the chemistry: B vitamins as coenzymes, iron in haemoglobin, calcium as scaffold and signal. Small doses, large consequences.' },
    { math: '5 · Water — the forgotten nutrient', note: 'The medium for everything above. Losses of just a few percent of body water measurably degrade performance and mood.' },
  ],
};

const balanceCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'intake', label: 'energy intake', min: 1200, max: 4000, step: 50, value: 2400, unit: 'kcal' },
    { name: 'bmr', label: 'basal metabolic rate', min: 1200, max: 2200, step: 25, value: 1650, unit: 'kcal' },
    { name: 'activity', label: 'activity multiplier', min: 1.2, max: 2.2, step: 0.05, value: 1.55, unit: '×' },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'estimated expenditure', expr: 'bmr*activity', unit: 'kcal' },
    { label: 'daily balance', expr: 'intake - bmr*activity', unit: 'kcal' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/nutrition-dietetics/foundational/nutrients-and-how-they-work">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Nutrition is the study of what food does inside the body. Its vocabulary is the nutrients:
    carbohydrates, proteins and fats supply energy and building material; vitamins and minerals run
    the chemistry; water is the medium. Every dietary argument you will ever read is, underneath,
    a claim about these six classes and how they interact.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the nutrient classes and what each is for" widget="equationStep" config={macroCfg} />

  <h2>Energy balance, made quantitative</h2>
  <p>
    The body obeys thermodynamics. Expenditure is mostly your basal metabolic rate — the cost of
    simply being alive — multiplied by an activity factor. Balance is intake minus expenditure, and
    it integrates daily: a sustained surplus is stored, a sustained deficit is drawn from reserves.
    Drag the sliders to see how fragile the balance actually is.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="intake vs expenditure — drag the sliders" widget="sliderLab" config={balanceCfg} />

  <h2>Why deficiency and excess look alike</h2>
  <p>
    Both ends of every nutrient curve are disease. Too little iron is anaemia; too much iron
    poisons the liver. Too little sodium is dangerous in endurance athletes; too much raises blood
    pressure. Nutrition's practical art is hitting a window, not maximising anything — which is why
    "more" is almost never the right advice for a nutrient.
  </p>

  <h2>From nutrients to diet</h2>
  <p>
    People eat foods, not nutrients, and foods deliver them in packages with fibre, matrices and
    interactions that change what the body absorbs. That gap between nutrient chemistry and dietary
    pattern is where most public confusion lives — and where the discipline of dietetics does its
    work: assessment first, then counselling grounded in evidence rather than fashion.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> ACEND accreditation standards; Institute of Medicine, <em>Dietary Reference Intakes</em>; Gibney et al., <em>Introduction to Human Nutrition</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
